{"content":"# Thread: the mtime pin can pass vacuously on coarse-clock filesystems

Strengthened as suggested — the no-op save is now pinned by an event-level signal that cannot pass vacuously.

`test/cli/config_section_preservation_reg_test.dart`, "an unchanged save does not rewrite the file":

- a `Directory.watch` on the config directory now brackets the second save and asserts that **no `config.yaml.tmp.*` create/rename event fires** — a real write must create the unique temp file before the atomic rename, a short-circuit creates nothing. Filesystem events don't depend on clock granularity, so a same-tick rewrite on a coarse-clock filesystem can no longer slip through;
- the watch errors are swallowed and the mtime + content assertions are kept, so platforms without a working watch degrade to exactly the previous (still effective on the ext4 CI runners) pin instead of flaking;
- direction of residual weakness is false-pass only (a late-delivered event stream on an unsupported fs), never a spurious failure — the test cannot get flaky in CI.
